{"command": "cd /testbed && sed -n '40,80p' specs/001-transactions/spec.md", "exit_code": 0, "stderr": "", "stdout": "**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** entries k1 (tags a,b), k2 (tags a), k3 (no tags), **When** invalidate(\"a\") is called, **Then** exactly k1 and k2 are removed, the return value is 2, k3 remains, and len(cache) reflects only live entries.\n2. **Given** key k with tags (\"a\"), **When** put(k, new_value, tags=(\"b\")) is called, **Then** the old tag membership \"a\" is removed; invalidate(\"a\") returns 0 and invalidate(\"b\") removes k.\n3. **Given** a cache at capacity, **When** new puts evict the least-recently-used entry carrying tag t, **Then** invalidate(t) no longer counts the evicted entry, and a later put of the same key with different tags is not affected by stale memberships of the evicted entry.\n4. **Given** a cache with no entries carrying tag t, **When** invalidate(t) is called, **Then** it returns 0 and state is unchanged.\n\n---\n\n### User Story 3 - Bounded Capacity with Strict Construction and Replacement Validation (Priority: P3)\n\nA library user bounds the cache with a positive integer maxsize; every value costs exactly one capacity slot regardless of object size. Invalid maxsize values (including booleans, zero, negatives, non-integers) are rejected at construction. An invalid replacement (bad tags on an existing key) leaves the old value, tags, and recency completely unchanged.\n\n**Why this priority**: Bounding and validation protect memory safety and contract integrity; they are verifiable once P1/P2 exist.\n\n**Independent Test**: Fully testable by constructing with invalid maxsize values (expect rejection), filling a small cache to capacity, observing eviction of the least-recently-used entry, and attempting invalid replacements on live entries.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** maxsize values 0, -1, True, False, 2.5, \"3\", **When** the cache is constructed, **Then** each is rejected (ValueError or TypeError) and no cache is created; positive integers (e.g. 1, 3) are accepted.\n2. **Given** a cache with maxsize 2 holding k1 (oldest) and k2, **When** put(k3) is called, **Then** k1 is evicted, len(cache) is 2, and get(k1) raises KeyError.\n3. **Given** a live entry k, **When** put(k, value, tags=(\"a\", 1)) is attempted (invalid tag), **Then** ValueError is raised and k retains its old value, old tags, and its prior recency position (a subsequent eviction evicts the least-recently-used entry, not k, if k was not the LRU).\n\n### Edge Cases\n\n- Empty tag iterable (default) - entry stored with no tags; cannot be removed by any invalidate(tag).\n- Duplicate tags in one put - deduplicated; membership counted once.\n- invalidate(tag) with a tag never used - returns 0, no error.\n- put with the same key and same value but different tags - old tag memberships replaced by new ones.\n- Eviction of an entry, then re-put of the same key - no stale tag membership from the evicted incarnation remains.\n- get on an entry refreshes its recency, protecting it from being the next eviction.\n- Unhashable keys - rejected with the standard unhashable-key error, state unchanged.\n- maxsize of 1 - every put evicts the previous entry (after recency refresh on overwrite, the overwritten key survives its own overwrite).\n\n## Requirements *(mandatory)*\n\n### Functional Requirements\n\n- **FR-001**: The feature MUST expose a new bounded tagged cache construct, TaggedCache, created with a single maxsize argument, in addition to (never replacing) all existing cachetools public APIs, which MUST remain behaviorally unchanged.\n- **FR-002**: put(key, value, tags=()) MUST insert a new entry or replace an existing one for the same key; keys MUST be hashable and unhashable keys MUST be rejected without state change.\n- **FR-003**: get(key) MUST return a detached deep copy of the stored value and MUST raise KeyError for missing keys; values MUST be deep-copied on input to put so that later mutation of the caller's original object never affects the cache.\n"}